#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151844 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/id #uuid "de1fa48e-853a-5aa4-ab32-f23540ef1dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14598" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $\\mathbb{Z}?$\n\n- $a \\not \\mid b$\n\n- $a \\,R\\, b$ if $a - b$ is a multiple of $7$\n\n- $a\\, R \\,b$ if $a \\equiv (b - 1) \\; (\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x \\in A,$ $x \\, R\\, x .$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y \\in A,$ if $x \\,R\\, y,$ then $y \\,R\\, x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ if $x \\,R\\, y$ and $y \\,R\\, z,$ then $x \\,R\\, z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $1 \\mid 1.$\n\n- Relation II is an equivalence relation. \n\n- It is reflexive because for all $a \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ we have that $a - a=0$ is a multiple of $7.$\n\n- It is symmetric because for all $a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ if $a - b$ is a multiple of $7,$ then $b - a=-(a-b)$ is also a multiple of $7.$\n\n- It is transitive because for all $a, b, c \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ if $a - b=7n$ and $b - c=7m,$ where $n, m$ are arbitrary integers, then\n\n$$\na - c = (a - b) + (b - c)=7n+7m=7(n+m)\n$$\n\nThis means that if $a - b$ is a multiple of $7$ and $b - c$ is a multiple of $7$ then $a-c$ is also a multiple of $7.$\n\n- Relation III is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $1 \\not\\equiv (1 - 1) \\; (\\textrm{mod}\\:5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151845 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "968df6ec-e87e-5770-9c11-e7c8d8a27e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations on Finite Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "6b0a0a3a-7079-5798-b2b3-14f7165ade81" :question/math-academy-id "e-14599" :question/problem "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A=\\{\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma\\}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR=\\big\\{(\\alpha, \\alpha), (\\beta,\\beta), (\\gamma,\\gamma), (\\beta,\\gamma), (\\gamma,\\beta)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n\n- $R$ is symmetric\n\n- $R$ is transitive\n\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x \\in A,$ $x \\, R\\, x .$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y \\in A,$ if $x \\,R\\, y,$ then $y \\,R\\, x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ if $x \\,R\\, y$ and $y \\,R\\, z,$ then $x \\,R\\, z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive because $(\\alpha, \\alpha), (\\beta,\\beta), (\\gamma,\\gamma) \\in R.$$\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x) \\in R$ whenever $(x,y) \\in R.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in \\{\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma\\},$ if  $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,z)\\in R,$  then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Recall that it's sufficient to check only the cases when both pairs have different components: \n\n- $({\\color{blue}\\beta},{\\color{red}\\gamma}) \\in R$ and $({\\color{red}\\gamma},{\\color{blue}\\beta})  \\in R,$ and also $({\\color{blue}\\beta},{\\color{blue}\\beta}) \\in R$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- $({\\color{blue}\\gamma},{\\color{red}\\beta}) \\in R$ and $({\\color{red}\\beta},{\\color{blue}\\gamma})  \\in R,$ and also $({\\color{blue}\\gamma},{\\color{blue}\\gamma}) \\in R$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Since $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, it is an equivalence relation.$\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151870 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b70dd396-b01a-51e6-a52c-8ff729f48c45" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Standard Relation Is an Equivalence"}] :question/id #uuid "de6949c6-850c-511e-8bea-bb246972cda4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14695" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $\\mathcal{P}({B}),$ where $B=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.$\n\n$$\nX \\,R\\, Y \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad X \\subseteq Y\n$$\n\n Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n\n- $R$ is symmetric\n\n- $R$ is transitive\n\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x \\in A,$ $x \\, R\\, x .$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y \\in A,$ if $x \\,R\\, y,$ then $y \\,R\\, x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z \\in A,$ if $x \\,R\\, y$ and $y \\,R\\, z,$ then $x \\,R\\, z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive. For all $X \\in \\mathcal{P}({B}),$ $X \\, \\subseteq \\, X .$\n\n- The relation $R$ is not symmetric. For example, $\\{1\\} \\subseteq \\{1,2\\}$ but $\\{1,2\\}  \\not \\subseteq \\{1\\}.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive. For all $X,Y,Z \\in \\mathcal{P}({B}),$ if $X \\subseteq Y$ and $Y \\subseteq Z,$ then $X \\subseteq Z.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is not an equivalence relation since it's not symmetric.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b70dd396-b01a-51e6-a52c-8ff729f48c45" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Standard Relation Is an Equivalence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "943268f0-e1b4-513a-949a-2d21d36b144f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149557} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3b8483-7822-5961-8352-009c78743961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149558} {:answer/id #uuid "08f98ec1-18ee-57a6-969c-0d18c2628d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149559} {:answer/id #uuid "34dc1e82-49c3-5dba-b2c5-d8a52a7f4307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149560} {:answer/id #uuid "370eafa0-4bc5-59db-869d-c3a8e48711d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f98ec1-18ee-57a6-969c-0d18c2628d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149559} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3d5c8a-9c69-53c4-bf41-d1986e372cfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2a24748-f2b1-5dae-9e25-ddb47fe6a36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-214134" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A$ containing all possible triangles in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x≅y\n$$\n\nRecall that $x≅y$ means that $x$ and $y$ are congruent.\n\nWhich of the following are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n- $R$ is symmetric\n- $R$ is transitive\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive. For all $x\\in A,$ we have $x≅x.$ Every triangle is congruent with itself.\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric. For all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x≅y,$ then $y≅x.$ To see why, note that if we can find a sequence of rigid motions in the plane that maps $x$ to $y$ (implying $x≅y),$ then we can always find a sequence of rigid motions that maps $y$ to $x.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive. For all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x≅y,$ and $y≅z,$ then $x≅z.$ To see why, note that if we can find a sequence of rigid motions in the plane that maps $x$ to $y$ (implying $x≅y)$ and another sequence that maps $y$ to $z,$ then we can always find a sequence that maps $x$ to $z.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "968df6ec-e87e-5770-9c11-e7c8d8a27e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations on Finite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d66d2717-83d7-51b0-a227-fa1fc6809409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149569} {:answer/id #uuid "30eef23f-1a55-5ac4-8a57-9c63c6f949e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149570} {:answer/id #uuid "5b128d60-e594-5aa5-bff5-a1e2fa17357d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149571} {:answer/id #uuid "66c8be3b-ec27-54c3-a77d-bf9d20dd0b93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149572} {:answer/id #uuid "7410e289-bd9b-5799-8292-33605aba5faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d66d2717-83d7-51b0-a227-fa1fc6809409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149569} :answer-field/id #uuid "692a1b0c-9534-5975-bd7a-3a92a99093ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14a51040-e179-5b66-9e0b-fca0e478f23c" :question/math-academy-id "q-214171" :question/problem "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={0,1,2}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(0,2),(2,0)}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n- $R$ is symmetric\n- $R$ is transitive\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* reflexive because  $(0,0)\\notin R.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x)\\in R$ whenever $(x,y)\\in R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* transitive because $(0,2)\\in R$ and $(2,0)\\in R,$ but $(0,0)\\notin R$\n\n- The relation is *not* an equivalence relation since it's neither reflexive nor transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69c8f651-7fad-58ab-9225-eb16c24dc65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421344} {:answer/id #uuid "90760cd8-7871-5634-b9b5-3531a983c1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421354} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b3f0ee-7ced-5feb-9405-387aa70e3545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421361} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5dc06e-ed8a-53d3-bee4-a48767f1eff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421378} {:answer/id #uuid "fec83f8f-fd76-54ba-94a1-1cd6f83935d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc5dc06e-ed8a-53d3-bee4-a48767f1eff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421378} :answer-field/id #uuid "763d9cb9-42d8-5b2a-baa3-fc94d026c898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c07a7ee-9fb6-5236-b864-38ec61e9b0cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-214241" :question/problem "Which of the following are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n\n- $A⊄B$\n- $|A|\\ne |B|$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- The relation \"$⊄$\" is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since ${2,3}⊄{2}$ but ${2}⊂{2,3}.$\n\n- The relation \"$\\ne $\" for the cardinalities of sets is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive since $|{2}|=|{2}|.$\n\n- The relation \"$⊇$\" is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since $N⊇{1}$ but ${1}⊉N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040a02e2-1bb9-514c-903f-81f6a2d7ea14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421315} {:answer/id #uuid "17261494-8ee1-5a6c-a36e-65e458355a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421318} {:answer/id #uuid "18e496f1-8b52-5668-a3c0-762eb1ea9fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421319} {:answer/id #uuid "62992426-d8cb-559d-b581-acc3f07854b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421341} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c7b953-d54f-55fc-9672-1c5535f4bd2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18e496f1-8b52-5668-a3c0-762eb1ea9fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421319} :answer-field/id #uuid "f758d976-bcdb-5e5f-af93-d6835069d66d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c8d639d-4ba9-5766-b837-cc9cfbce29a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-214245" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n\n- $A\\,R\\,B$ if $A\\cap B=∅$\n- $A⊃B$\n- $A=B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${1}\\cap {1}\\ne ∅.$\n\n- Relation II is also not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${1}⊅{1}.$\n\n- Relation III is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2224d9bb-0faf-5fc9-b610-28aaddc41507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421321} {:answer/id #uuid "914a9fe7-3947-5a9f-92ed-734d047aebca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421355} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e40a81-ea5a-5e10-8335-f943e082f51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421366} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6d8781-5676-564f-b9b9-dac7cffccc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421371} {:answer/id #uuid "ce69759e-4547-5b71-b4e3-eb3dc5f1b96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7e40a81-ea5a-5e10-8335-f943e082f51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421366} :answer-field/id #uuid "36c1e14d-f406-53c3-919e-0c1fd156cc01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3f6ced1-e3e7-51b2-bdb3-c0561413aee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-214246" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $N?$\n\n- $a\\,R\\,b$ if $a-b$ is a multiple of $2$\n- $a∣b$\n- $a\\,R\\,b$ if $3∣(a+b)$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is an equivalence relation. \n\n- It is reflexive because for all $a\\in N,$ we have that $a-a=0$ is a multiple of $2.$\n\n- It is symmetric because for all $a,b\\in N,$ if $a-b$ is a multiple of $2,$ then $b-a$ is also a multiple of $2.$\n\n- It is transitive because for all $a,b,c\\in N,$ if $a-b$ is a multiple of $2$ and $b-c$ is a multiple of $2,$ then\n\n$$\na-c=(a-b)+(b-c)\n$$\n\nis also a multiple of $2.$\n\n- Relation II is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since $3∣6$ but $6∤3.$\n\n- Relation III is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive since $3∤(1+1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af48504-6f1c-5b90-87ab-46f546fb2eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421329} {:answer/id #uuid "6d984c53-eae2-5d52-bffe-bead8d751ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/